<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Every sym_* value is untranslatable notation, and must actually *be* notation: an
      English word in a symbol slot renders in English at titleMedium beside a fully
      translated unit name, and MissingTranslation cannot see it because the string is
      declared untranslatable. "long ton", "ton", "cup" and "turn" each shipped that way
      until they became LT, ST, c and tr.

      Known and deliberately left: the whole time category leans on English abbreviations —
      yr, mo, wk, and the invented c (century), dec (decade) and fn (fortnight), plus lea
      for league — because these units have no international symbol to reach for. They are
      not defensible as "the same in every language", so the rule above does not really
      cover them. They are left alone rather than half-fixed: translating c, dec, fn and lea
      while yr, mo and wk stay English would be a worse inconsistency than the present one,
      and doing the category properly means making seven or more sym_* translatable and
      adding eleven locales each, behind a fatal MissingTranslation gate, which is not a
      change to land beside a first submission. The cost of leaving it is bounded: the chip
      is opaque on its own, but ConverterScreen renders the fully localised unit name from
      UnitNames.nameRes in the same card, so the unit is never actually ambiguous. Fix the
      category as a unit, after the first release.
    -->
